    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "## About Freddie Mac\n",
        "\n",
        "The data originates from Freddie Mac, a group created by Congress in an effort to make mortgages more accessible to Americans. This accessibility is derived from Freddie Mac's mortgage purchases. Lenders sell mortgage loans to Freddie Mac, who in turn uses the assets as securities sold to investors. Securities give one the right to something else– a financial contract. In this case, mortgages as securities give investors a monthly stream of income for decades. As a main purchaser of mortgages, Freddie Mac has a large amount of mortgage data across the entire US. These data points are ideal for the Home Loan Adviser model.\n",
        "\n",
        "## About the Raw Data\n",
        "\n",
        "The data is provided by Freddie Mac in zip files by year. The download is available [here](https://claritydownload.fmapps.freddiemac.com/CRT/#/sflld). A guide is also provided [here](https://www.freddiemac.com/fmac-resources/research/pdf/user_guide.pdf). Each year of the SFLL data is composed of quarters, which are provided in zip files as well. Each quarter contains two files: one file contains loan origination data and the other contains the performance history of each loan. Their \"key\" is a column named `loan_sequence_number` to be used for joining. This key is renamed `loan_id`.\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bash}\n",
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "#--to extract automatically (on mac)-- \n",
        "cd ~/Downloads\n",
        "unzip \"historical_data_*.zip\"\n",
        "\n",
        "#-- remove the main zip from folder and re-run to extract quarters--\n",
        "unzip \"historical_data_*.zip\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Once the nested zip files were extracted, there were a total of 40 files. The raw data is in `.txt` format separated by `|` vertical bars, or \"pipes\", instead of commas or tabs. There are no headers, so all columns were named upon importing.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FreddieMac-Raw%20data.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.card .border-secondary}\n",
        "## Data Dictionary\n",
        "\n",
        "::: panel-tabset\n",
        "## Origination Data"
      ],
      "id": "41ed57d7"
    },
